most powerful in the  region but it accounts for only about 30  percent of the area's naval tonnage in  other words there is competition and so  visualpolitik fans this time we are not  going to talk to you about the big  players from countries such as japan  south korea australia taiwan or china  itself no in this video we want to focus  on two new emerging military powers in  the asia pacific two that may still be a  long way from the first division but  whose situation in a few years could  change radically what exactly are these  countries destined to become the next  military powers in the region and just  how influential are they likely to  become could they be the counterweights  that will determine the future balance  of power between china and the west well  let's get into it  [Music]  take a look at this band  what you are looking at is the south  china sea with the main territorial  claims of the countries around its shore  as you can see it is a huge puzzle the  dispute over these waters is so crazy  that up to four countries are claiming  the same piece of the pie at exactly the  same time and the problem is that china  seems less and less willing to deal with  all this mess after all it is the  largest and strongest country in the  area so why not impose its will why not  take over all the territory it considers  its own and control all the natural  resources that these waters may hold  well do you know what that's exactly  what xi jinping seems to think and that  is precisely why the chinese government  is increasingly raising the tone and its  demands on these territorial claims of  course china's neighbours aren't sitting  idly by and they've said to themselves  we'd better watch out  the people's republic of china wants to  take the lead the problem is that its  neighbours are not willing to go along  with its criteria because it is one  thing for china to be the strongest  country and another for it to become a  dominant power so while between 2003 and  2018 china increased its military budget  by 621 percent vietnam increased theirs  by 687  and other countries such as indonesia or  thailand which increased theirs by 255  percent and 264 percent respectively now  want to step on the accelerator even  more  let's just say that in the face of  china's military escalation more and  more countries in the area have come to  the same conclusion if we don't prepare  ourselves this could end up disastrous  for us  [Applause]  an idea that the current russian  invasion of ukraine has proven to be  neither an exaggeration nor an unfounded  fear  china has fully militarized three  islands in the south china sea us  admiral says  vietnam builds military muscle to face  china  u.s seen bolstering military links in  southeast asia to counter china did you  imagine that vietnam for example the new  rising star was increasing its military  budget even faster than china itself  doesn't all this deserve its own visual  politic video well listen up  history that won't be repeated  vietnam knows all too well what it means  to face a foreign power throughout the  20th century it faced france united  states what can i say and also china  itself so if there's one thing the  vietnamese are clear about it's that  they have to be prepared for any  eventuality  [Music]  precisely for this reason the vietnamese  government included military  reinforcement as one of the fundamental  axes of its famous 1986 doi moy policy  to reform its economic system and  gradually adopt a model of state  capitalism similar to that of china it  included military reform among the  objectives because up to that time  vietnam's armed forces were more like a  series of fragmented ill-equipped and  poorly trained military groups with  little correlation amongst themselves  however the collapse of the soviet union  put the brakes on this change vietnam  was a very poor country and the soviet  collapse only made things worse so  military modernization had to wait  [Music]  nevertheless with the arrival of the new  country and its opening up to the world  the vietnamese economy began to undergo  a huge revolution to the point of  becoming one of the fastest growing  economies in the world as early as 2006  the 10th national congress of the  communist party of vietnam set the goal  of turning the country into a  full-fledged naval power both  economically and militarily  [Music]  vietnam has thus begun to equip itself  with relatively modern weaponry mainly  from russia although among its  inventories we also find south korean  anti-submarine frigates french  helicopters and israeli drones not to  mention an increasing amount of u.s  equipment because despite their history  relations between the united states and  vietnam have been growing closer in  recent years  yes vietnam is much poorer than china  and its military budget is still a  fraction of that of its northern  neighbor but if there's one thing  ukraine has proven it's that you don't  need to be the strongest if you have  certain capabilities and above all if  you get good friends  japan vietnam signed defence transfer  deal amid china worries  us vp kamala harris makes historic visit  to vietnam we all know that the vietnam  war has already shown what a trap this  country can be for a foreign power so  hanoi's strategy is to increase the cost  to china of trying to take over the  vietnamese islets in the south china sea  as much as possible this explains for  example its determination to make their  submarine fleet currently with six units  in service one of the strategic elements  of its defense it also explains why it  has devoted considerable resources to  reinforcing the defenses of the spratzly  archipelago where it has rocket  launchers and attack systems with  sufficient range to attack chinese  controlled islets and neutralize their  airfields and radar systems  this is not only a political or  territorial issue but also and perhaps  above all an economic one you see  vietnam is experiencing a huge economic  boom and more and more companies are  seeing it as a possible alternative to  china or at least as a way of reducing  their exposure to the asian giant so  it's not unusual to come across news  stories like this samsung invests 920  million in vietnam plant expansion apple  supplier foxconn to build 270 million  dollar plant in vietnam amid u.s china  tensions  lg display raises investment in vietnam  factory by 1.4 billion local government  the lego group announces plans to build  new factory in vietnam to support  long-term growth it is precisely for  this reason that controlling and  enduring the safety and integrity of the  waters through which vietnam exports all  these goods is fundamental for  continuing to attract foreign direct  investment but visual politics fans  vietnam is far from the only country  that is starting to move  [Music]  guns versus butter  [Music]  if there's one country in southeast asia  that aspires to become a major player in  the region it's none other than  indonesia the reasons the same as we  have already seen in the case of vietnam  the threat from china and the need to  ensure control of the waters through  which it's growing exports to the rest  of the world transit the fact is that  jakarta seems to have had enough of the  constant chinese military incursions in  its waters this explains why they have  wanted to put their foot down at the  very least make some noise in the media  indonesia's 125 billion air naval  splurge may mean 50 warships at sea in  the next two years amidst incursions by  china into indonesia's extended economic  zone defence minister parabolo sabantio  is looking for major investment in  military gear the new story is tricky  because it is a long-term investment  figure but these kinds of announcements  are a way of saying hey we are here and  over the next few years we're going to  take the military issue much more  seriously than we have done so far until  now military matters as in most of the  countries in the area had been secondary  and in fact the indonesian military  hardware is not only old very old but  also a large part of it is in a very bad  state  for example in july 2020 a 1970s landing  ship purchased from east germany sank on  them in 2021 they lost a 40 year old  submarine in torpedo practice resulting  in the death of 53 sailors what's more  accidents with trainer and light attack  aircraft are the order of the day  but now the idea is that all of this  will change among the materials that  could arrive in the coming years we find  equipment such as the french rafale  lacist versions of the usf-15 and the  new south korean kf-21 fighter in whose  development program indonesia is  participating but above all this  country's military strategy has a  priority the development of what is  known as a green water navy a term that  refers to naval forces specialized in  coastal defense which on one hand would  allow it to control the national  territory don't forget that we are  talking about a country that has more  than 17 000 islands and or the other  hinder any ambition that china might  have over them the idea is a bit like  what we have seen in ukraine a much much  stronger power is going to think twice  before daring to get into a hornet's  list like this  this is what is known as operational  psychological deterrence a strategy that  involves creating a maritime defense  that makes any enemy incursion have a  sufficiently high cost so that the  potential invader completely abandons  his plans in other words the cost to  benefit ratio does not pay off  but to better understand what is behind  this objective we can take a look for  example at the natuna islands 272  islands under indonesian control that  are nevertheless within the line of the  new points claimed by china these  islands also come with a gift it is  estimated that their sub-soil contains  trillions of cubic meters of gas in fact  the enormous amount of gas estimated to  be in place could turn them into one of  the largest gas fields in the world and  i repeat they are claimed by china as  their own i'm sure you now have a better  understanding of the indonesian  determination to develop this green  water armada as rapidly as possible the  idea is to equip 274 ships 10 air combat  squadrons and 12 new diesel submarines  among other equipment  indonesia looks to triple submarine  fleet after chinese incursions of course  there is also a problem many experts  point out that chinese precision guided  missiles along with other technological  advances such as drones themselves would  allow beijing to attack and mop up the  area before penetrating indonesia's  exclusive economic zone this is what is  known as war from home  be that as it may vietnam and indonesia  are two examples of emerging economies  that are now beginning to take this  issue seriously and keep in mind that  between the two countries they will soon  have more than 400 million inhabitants  and an ever-increasing economic  development in other words we are not  talking about a small potential now  let's take a look at this other map  remember what we were saying earlier  about the tonnage of naval forces in the  region china is becoming a superpower  but it is a surrounded power that would  struggle to achieve dominance look at  the map look at south korea japan the  philippines taiwan vietnam and indonesia  together they would form a kind of  security barrier capable of greatly  limiting china's ambitions and would  force china to pay attention to too many  different fronts that's why improving  the capabilities of vietnam indonesia  and the philippines can be so important  geopolitically especially when these  countries are forging closer ties with  the united states south korea and japan  as the case seems to be the game is far  from over the war in ukraine has spurred  the desire of all these countries to  improve their capabilities to avoid  becoming a future ukraine makes sense  doesn't it but now it's your turn which  countries do you think will be the  future military powers of the world how  far will china's power go will it become  a dominant power in the asia pacific  region how far will this military race  go leave us your impressions below in  the comments and if you found this video  interesting don't forget to like it and  subscribe to visualpolitik if you  haven't already done so thank you very  very much once again for being here all  the best i'll see you next time  [Music]  you

